A = {
'vertigo-dizziness-2569': dict(
  title='Vertigo and Dizziness: Causes, Symptoms and Care (2026 Update)',
  excerpt='What causes vertigo, how it differs from ordinary light-headedness, and which danger signs mean you should go to hospital — with advice from HappiWell Clinic, Thung Khru.',
  seo_title='Vertigo & Dizziness: Causes, Symptoms & Care (2026) | HappiWell',
  seo_description='What causes vertigo, how it differs from light-headedness, and the danger signs that need a hospital. Advice from HappiWell Medical Clinic, Thung Khru, Bangkok.',
  blocks=[
    'Suddenly the world is spinning and you cannot keep your balance. Many people panic and fear something serious. In fact, most vertigo has a harmless, treatable cause — but sometimes it is a warning sign of an emergency that needs a hospital. This article explains what causes vertigo and how to tell when it is dangerous.',
    'How is vertigo different from ordinary dizziness?',
    'Vertigo is the feeling that you or your surroundings are spinning when nothing is actually moving. Ordinary dizziness is more often a feeling of light-headedness, unsteadiness or poor balance, without the spinning sensation.',
    'The most common cause',
    'The most common cause of vertigo is <b>benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV)</b>, which happens when tiny calcium crystals in the inner ear move out of place. It causes sudden spinning when you change your head position — looking down or up, turning over in bed or sitting up. Episodes are usually short, lasting from a few seconds to a few minutes.',
    'Other causes include inflammation of the balance nerve or balance organs in the inner ear, and Ménière’s disease, which often comes with a blocked feeling or ringing in the ear.',
    'Common symptoms',
    ['A spinning, swaying feeling or loss of balance', 'Nausea and vomiting', 'Symptoms that come and go, often linked to head movements (in BPPV)'],
    'Danger signs: go to hospital immediately',
    'Although most vertigo is not dangerous, it can sometimes be a sign of stroke. Remember <b>BE FAST</b> and go to hospital immediately if you have any of these together with dizziness or vertigo:',
    ['<b>B (Balance)</b>: sudden loss of balance or staggering', '<b>E (Eyes)</b>: sudden blurred vision or loss of vision', '<b>F (Face)</b>: drooping face — one side of the mouth droops when smiling', '<b>A (Arms)</b>: weakness or numbness in an arm or leg on one side', '<b>S (Speech)</b>: slurred speech, or speech others cannot understand', '<b>T (Time)</b>: if you have any of these symptoms, go to hospital immediately — every minute counts'],
    'Also see a doctor immediately if you are confused, have trouble speaking or understanding speech, or have a sudden severe headache unlike any before.',
    'Self-care during an episode',
    ['Sit or lie down straight away when you feel dizzy, to lower the risk of falling', 'Avoid movements that trigger symptoms', 'Older adults or anyone at risk of falling should use a walking stick for support'],
    'How is it treated?',
    'BPPV can be treated by a doctor with specific head-positioning manoeuvres (canalith repositioning) that move the crystals back to where they do not cause symptoms. Most people improve after 1–2 sessions. Even after successful treatment, it can come back in the future.',
    'Vertigo checks at HappiWell Clinic',
    f'At {CLINIC} in Thung Khru, a doctor can assess vertigo and dizziness. If there are any of the danger signs above, we will refer you to a suitably equipped hospital straight away for your safety.',
    BOOK,
    'Frequently asked questions',
    '<b>Can vertigo go away on its own?</b><br>BPPV sometimes clears up by itself within a few weeks to months, but treatment by a doctor helps it clear much faster.',
    '<b>If I have had BPPV before, can it come back?</b><br>Yes. Around half of people who were successfully treated may have it again within a year.',
    '<b>Which is more dangerous, ordinary dizziness or vertigo?</b><br>It does not depend on the type, but on whether you have worrying symptoms as well, such as the BE FAST signs. Those always need a hospital, whatever kind of dizziness you have.',
    '<b>What should older adults who often feel dizzy watch out for?</b><br>Be especially careful about falls, and see a doctor to find the cause, because older adults are more likely to have several causes at once.',
    'Summary',
    'Most vertigo is caused by BPPV, which is not dangerous and can be treated by a doctor with head-positioning manoeuvres. But if you have BE FAST symptoms such as a drooping face, weak limbs or slurred speech, go to hospital immediately, as these may be signs of a stroke.',
    'References',
    None,
    '<i>This article is for general information and is not personal medical advice. If you have any BE FAST symptoms, go to hospital immediately.</i>',
  ]),

'hives-urticaria-2569': dict(
  title='Hives (Urticaria): Causes, Symptoms and Care (2026 Update)',
  excerpt='What causes hives, how dangerous they are, how to look after yourself and which signs of a severe allergic reaction mean you must go to hospital — with advice from HappiWell Clinic, Thung Khru.',
  seo_title='Hives (Urticaria): Causes, Symptoms & Care (2026) | HappiWell',
  seo_description='What causes hives, how dangerous they are, self-care, and the signs of a severe allergic reaction that need a hospital. Advice from HappiWell Medical Clinic, Thung Khru, Bangkok.',
  blocks=[
    'Suddenly your skin breaks out in raised red welts that itch all over, and you have no idea why. Hives are very common — about 1 in 5 people get them at least once in their lives. Most cases are not dangerous or contagious, but sometimes they are a sign of a severe allergic reaction that needs a hospital immediately. This article explains what hives are, how to care for them and how to tell when they are dangerous.',
    'What are hives?',
    'Hives (urticaria) are raised red or pink welts that appear suddenly on the skin and are usually very itchy. They range from small spots to large patches that join together. Hives are not contagious, and most go away on their own.',
    'Common causes',
    ['Allergy to certain foods or medicines', 'Insect bites or stings', 'Very hot or cold weather, or heavy sweating', 'Stress', 'Tight clothing', 'Some NSAID painkillers'],
    'Sometimes no clear cause can be found. Especially for chronic hives lasting more than 6 weeks, the doctor may need to run tests to look for a hidden cause, such as thyroid disease.',
    'How do ordinary hives differ from hives in a severe allergic reaction?',
    '<b>This is the most important thing to know.</b> Hives that appear suddenly can be part of a severe allergic reaction (anaphylaxis), a life-threatening emergency. Go to hospital immediately if you have hives together with:',
    ['Difficulty breathing or wheezing', 'Swelling of the lips, tongue or throat', 'Dizziness or feeling faint', 'Severe nausea or vomiting', 'Palpitations or a fast heartbeat'],
    'If you have an adrenaline auto-injector (epinephrine autoinjector), use it immediately, and still go to hospital even if you feel better, because a severe reaction can return within a few hours.',
    'Ordinary chronic hives without any of the symptoms above do not carry a risk of a sudden severe allergic reaction.',
    'Self-care for ordinary hives',
    ['Avoid anything you suspect is a trigger', 'Wear loose, comfortable clothing', 'Avoid very hot water or long warm showers', 'Take non-drowsy antihistamines as advised by a pharmacist or doctor'],
    'How is it treated?',
    'If there are no complications or danger signs, doctors usually recommend starting with a daily non-drowsy antihistamine. If that does not work, the doctor may adjust the dose or change the medicine. For severe chronic hives that ordinary antihistamines cannot control, specialist injections may be considered under a specialist’s care.',
    'Hives care at HappiWell Clinic',
    f'At {CLINIC} in Thung Khru, a doctor can examine and treat hives. If there are signs of a severe allergic reaction, we will refer you to a suitably equipped hospital straight away.',
    BOOK,
    'Frequently asked questions',
    '<b>Do hives go away on their own?</b><br>Most do. Some cases take up to 6 weeks. If they last longer, they are considered chronic hives and you should see a doctor.',
    '<b>Are hives contagious?</b><br>No. They are the body’s reaction to a trigger, not an infection.',
    '<b>I get hives often. Should I be tested for the cause?</b><br>If they last more than 6 weeks, see a doctor to look for a hidden cause, as they are sometimes linked to other conditions such as thyroid disease.',
    '<b>Antihistamines have not cleared my hives. What should I do?</b><br>Go back to your doctor to adjust the dose or change the medicine. Do not buy more medicine on your own without advice.',
    'Summary',
    'Hives are very common, and most are not dangerous and go away on their own. But if you also have difficulty breathing, swelling of the lips or throat, or feel faint, go to hospital immediately, as this may be a life-threatening severe allergic reaction.',
    'References',
    None,
    '<i>This article is for general information and is not personal medical advice. If you have hives with difficulty breathing or swollen lips, go to hospital immediately.</i>',
  ]),

'allergic-rhinitis-sinusitis-2569': dict(
  title='Allergic Rhinitis vs Sinusitis: Differences, Symptoms and Care (2026 Update)',
  excerpt='How allergic rhinitis and sinusitis differ, how to tell which one you have, how to look after yourself and when to see a doctor — with advice from HappiWell Clinic, Thung Khru.',
  seo_title='Allergic Rhinitis vs Sinusitis: What’s the Difference? (2026) | HappiWell',
  seo_description='How allergic rhinitis and sinusitis differ, how to tell which you have, self-care and when to see a doctor. Advice from HappiWell Medical Clinic, Thung Khru, Bangkok.',
  blocks=[
    'A blocked nose, runny nose and frequent sneezing — many people wonder whether it is an allergy or sinusitis, especially in Bangkok with PM2.5 dust and frequent weather changes. This article explains the differences, the symptoms to look for and how to look after yourself.',
    'What is allergic rhinitis?',
    'Allergic rhinitis happens when the immune system overreacts to allergens in the air, such as pollen, pet hair, mould, dust mites or PM2.5 dust. The body releases histamine, which causes allergy symptoms.',
    'Symptoms of allergic rhinitis',
    ['Blocked nose, frequent sneezing and runny nose', 'Itchy nose, throat and eyes', 'Red, watery eyes', 'Headache and a feeling of pressure in the sinuses', 'Dark circles under the eyes'],
    'A key clue is that <b>allergic rhinitis usually comes with itchy eyes and an itchy nose</b>, whereas sinusitis rarely causes itching.',
    'What is sinusitis, and how is it different?',
    'Sinusitis is inflammation and swelling of the sinuses around the nose, which stops mucus draining properly so it builds up. It often follows a common cold or poorly controlled allergies. Common symptoms are:',
    ['Blocked nose and difficulty breathing through the nose', 'Thick yellow or green mucus, or mucus dripping down the back of the throat', 'Pain or pressure around the eyes, cheeks, nose or forehead, especially when bending forward', 'Headache, sometimes with toothache'],
    'How to look after yourself',
    ['Avoid allergens you suspect are triggers', 'Rinse your nose with saline to clear mucus and allergens', 'Use antihistamines or nasal sprays as advised by a doctor or pharmacist', 'Wear a mask in areas with smoke, dust or high PM2.5'],
    'When should you see a doctor?',
    'If your symptoms get worse, do not improve with self-care, or last more than 2 weeks, see a doctor. If you have frequent or chronic sinusitis, you may need tests to find an underlying allergy and plan long-term treatment.',
    'Allergy and sinusitis care at HappiWell Clinic',
    f'At {CLINIC} in Thung Khru, a doctor can examine and treat allergic rhinitis and sinusitis — no need to travel to a big hospital. Booking is easy, with no long wait.',
    BOOK,
    'Frequently asked questions',
    '<b>Can allergic rhinitis be cured?</b><br>Symptoms can usually be well controlled by avoiding triggers and using medicine, but it is not completely cured in everyone.',
    '<b>Does sinusitis always need antibiotics?</b><br>Not always. Most sinusitis is caused by colds or allergies, which antibiotics do not help. Let a doctor decide whether you need them.',
    '<b>Does PM2.5 dust really make allergies worse?</b><br>Yes. PM2.5 is one of the triggers that can worsen respiratory allergies. Wear a protective mask when dust levels are high.',
    '<b>How is a sinus headache different from a migraine?</b><br>A sinus headache usually comes with a blocked nose and facial pressure that gets worse when you bend forward. If you are unsure, let a doctor check.',
    'Summary',
    'Allergic rhinitis and sinusitis have similar symptoms but different causes and clues. Allergic rhinitis usually causes itchy eyes and nose, while sinusitis mainly causes facial pressure and thick mucus. If your symptoms do not improve or keep coming back, see a doctor to find the cause and plan the right care.',
    'References',
    None,
    '<i>This article is for general information and is not personal medical advice. See a doctor if your symptoms do not improve or keep coming back.</i>',
  ]),

'nhso-quota-cut-2569': dict(
  title='NHSO Keeps Cutting Gold Card Benefits — Now a Weekly Quota of Just Dozens. What Does It Mean for Patients and Clinics?',
  excerpt='A timeline of how Thailand’s NHSO has reduced Gold Card benefits and payments to innovative service units: from 320 to 180 baht per visit, from 2 visits a month to 2 a year, and now weekly quotas.',
  seo_title='NHSO Cuts Gold Card “30 Baht Anywhere” Benefits: What Clinics Face',
  seo_description='Timeline of NHSO cuts to the Gold Card “30 baht anywhere” scheme — from 320 to 180 baht per visit and new weekly quotas — and how patients and clinics are affected.',
  blocks=[
    'If you regularly use your Gold Card (Universal Coverage) at a clinic near home, this affects you directly. Over the past year, Thailand’s National Health Security Office (NHSO) has changed the conditions for “innovative” Gold Card service units (medical clinics, pharmacies, dental clinics, Thai traditional medicine clinics and others) three times. Every change has gone in the same direction: fewer benefits, less money and more burden for both patients and service units.',
    'Timeline of the actual cuts',
    '<b>December 2025</b>: the NHSO suddenly changed contract terms. Instead of paying 320 baht per visit for up to 2 visits a month, it now pays just 180 baht per visit for 2 visits a <b>year</b> — cutting both the payment and the number of visits at once. It also required service units to lodge a 10,000-baht guarantee and pass an audit of every medical record, with money clawed back immediately if any error was found.',
    '<b>January 2026</b>: the NHSO introduced a QR-code booking system that must be used within 6 hours, adding hassle for patients who have to rush to use it and for clinics that had to reorganise their queues entirely.',
    '<b>July 2026</b>: most recently, the NHSO changed the system again to allocate services on a <b>weekly</b> basis, based on data going back to March. As a result, many service units now receive a quota of only a few dozen patients a week. This means that even if your Gold Card rights are fully intact, you may arrive at a clinic and be unable to use them because that week’s quota is already full.',
    'What the NHSO says',
    'The NHSO says the latest change aligns budget allocation with each unit’s actual services and manages the budget efficiently towards the end of the fiscal year. It stresses that Gold Card rights have not been cancelled, but that an innovative unit’s service quota for a given week may be full. It advises calling before travelling, or calling the 1330 hotline.',
    'That sounds reasonable in theory, but in practice it shifts the whole burden onto patients, who must check the quota before every visit, and onto clinics, which must hope they get enough quota for the patients who actually come. The Gold Card system was meant to make care easier to access, not harder.',
    'Why this has a real impact, not just numbers on paper',
    ['What happens to people who fall ill more than twice a year at an innovative service unit, when illness does not follow a quota?', 'Clinics that planned their income around 320 baht per visit must suddenly adjust to just 180 baht, while the cost of medicines, supplies and staff has not fallen', 'Unpredictable weekly quotas mean neither patients nor clinics can plan ahead — there may be quota today and none tomorrow', 'The burden of checking rights before every visit falls on patients, especially older adults who are not comfortable with apps or phoning to ask'],
    'HappiWell Clinic continues to care fully for Gold Card patients',
    'Although we face the same limits as service units across the country, HappiWell Medical Clinic remains committed to caring for Gold Card patients in Thung Khru as well as the system allows. If you are worried about the quota, please message us on LINE or call before you travel, so you do not make a wasted trip.',
    'Summary',
    'Over the past year, the NHSO has changed the conditions for innovative service units three times, each time reducing payments to service units, reducing how often patients can use their rights and making care harder to access. Although the NHSO insists rights have not been cancelled, having to check the quota before every doctor’s visit is not what a universal health coverage system should look like. We hope the NHSO will review this approach and announce changes in advance, so that patients and service units can adapt, rather than making sudden changes again and again.',
    'References',
    ['<a href="https://www.hfocus.org/content/2025/12/36311">Hfocus – Dr Ratcharin criticises NHSO for changing innovative clinic criteria mid-stream (Thai)</a>', '<a href="https://www.hfocus.org/content/2025/12/36306">Hfocus – NHSO explains changes to Gold Card contract terms for innovative clinics (Thai)</a>', '<a href="https://www.hfocus.org/content/2026/07/38840">Hfocus – NHSO reallocates service numbers for innovative units, advises Gold Card users to call first (Thai)</a>'],
    '<i>This article reflects the clinic’s views and opinions, based on publicly available news sources as of the date of publication.</i>',
  ]),
}
